# Rank 1658 / CVE-2025-55182: the Flight reply deserializer resolved a
# client-supplied "$id:seg:seg" outline reference by walking the
# deserialized objects one segment at a time with a plain subscript, so a
# reference such as $3:constructor:constructor reached through the
# prototype chain to the Function constructor, and a crafted Promise-like
# .then resolves via the chunk wake path -- unauthenticated remote code
# execution on any Server Function endpoint (CVSS 10.0; the React blog
# post and Dynatrace's analysis name the missing attribute check). The
# walk in getOutlinedModel (packages/react-server/src/ReactFlightReplyServer.js)
# ran `value = value[path[i]]` with no own-property check, where path is
# reference.split(':') and reference is a slice of the reply model string;
# loadServerReference then handed metaData.id to resolveServerReference /
# requireModule with no typeof check. The fixing commit 7dc903cd29
# ("Patch FlightReplyServer with fixes from ReactFlightClient (#35277)",
# 822 lines in this file) gates both walk sites on
# `typeof value === 'object' && hasOwnProperty.call(value, name)`, makes
# loadServerReference reject a non-string id, and lets getChunk and
# resolveStream take only string FormData entries. Remediation verified
# present at the fixed revision. No pool correction.
#
# ATTENTION, two stacked gaps, rechecked and both re-measured.
#
# 1. Engine, frontend: a function whose signature carries Flow-only
# type syntax has no function context, and on this file the loss runs
# to the end of it. Isolated, the Flow-signature block below still
# yields six call nodes at module scope but zero
# analysis.function.context nodes, where the identical body behind a
# plain signature yields one -- no function-scoped binding can reach
# the walk at all. On the real parent revision it is worse: the
# 1180-line file yields 42 Call nodes and 13 Func nodes, every one at
# or before line 162 -- nothing after line 186 extracts, including the
# functions that carry no generic parameter (bindArgs at :337, getChunk
# at :518, createMap at :640, parseModelString at :916). `vyql query
# -call` reports 0 nodes for getOutlinedModel and for every `split`
# call, and `vyql match` on the file reports no source, no sink and no
# control. Three --cache off scans of packages/react-server (75
# javascript files) report 0 findings at the parent and 0 at the fix,
# and the same under the api, generic and library profiles (the library
# profile's 36 rows all sit in ReactFizzClassComponent.js); no rule can
# see this file. The loss is per-function in isolation -- a plain
# sibling after one generic declaration extracts -- but the real file's
# run of consecutive generic declarations takes the parser's recovery
# with it, so the practical loss is file-wide. Routing Flow-annotated
# .js through the TypeScript grammar (the extension switch already sends
# .ts there with the same walker) is the correction, and it stays a
# design call, not a mechanical one: pragma detection versus error
# retry, grammar choice, and a scan-wide change in extraction volume on
# every Flow-typed library. ENGINE-GAPS.md entry unchanged.
#
# 2. Vocabulary, provenance: the segments come from
# reference.split(':') where reference is a slice of the reply model
# string, and nothing labels the delimiter-separated names of a wire
# format as untrusted -- the family ranks 1518, 1584 and 1613 share, and
# rank 1725 measures the read arm of it again (code.ReflectionInvoke
# has a Java emitter only). Flattening the signature to plain
# javascript isolates this half from the first: the unguarded walk then
# has its function context, `reference.split` and `value[path[i]]` both
# extracted, and still reports nothing, so no source or sink exists for
# it. A guard-free presence binding over split+subscript walks is
# unshippable on the measured reachable set -- 15 of 168 split-carrying
# files on next.js, 38 of 266 on lime-survey, 35 of 181 on microweber,
# all vendored browser libraries reading trusted config paths.
#
# The control half is the one piece that works, and only through the
# frontend fact. On the flattened pair the own-property check makes
# `own_property_key_guard=true` fire 0 -> 1 (measured with a probe
# binding emitting core.PrototypePollutionGuard from that fact), which
# is what a future source/sink pair needs for its `unless`. The shipped
# ownPropertyCheckGuard binding does not reach this spelling: it
# matches Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call, `.hasOwnProperty(key)`
# and Object.hasOwn, and the fix's receiverless
# `hasOwnProperty.call(value, name)` -- hasOwnProperty imported from
# 'shared/hasOwnProperty', the form MDN recommends for null-prototype
# receivers -- matches none of the three. Widening a control binding is
# a suppression change with no finding here to measure it against, so
# nothing is shipped.
#
# The first and third blocks carry the signature the real file has and
# pin the extraction gap (zero function contexts against one for the
# flattened twin); the second and fourth flatten it and pin the
# vocabulary gap (one function context, the split and the subscript
# both extracted, and still nothing fires). All four reject
# VYQL-INJ-013. When Flow-annotated .js extracts and untrusted-name
# provenance lands, the unguarded walk must flip to expect.
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1658: the outline walk follows the prototype chain"
  lang javascript
  file packages/react-server/src/ReactFlightReplyServer.js
  reject VYQL-INJ-013
  code
  ```
  function getOutlinedModel<T>(
    response: Response,
    reference: string,
    map: (response: Response, model: any) => T,
  ): T {
    const path = reference.split(':');
    const id = parseInt(path[0], 16);
    const chunk = getChunk(response, id);
    switch (chunk.status) {
      case INITIALIZED:
        let value = chunk.value;
        for (let i = 1; i < path.length; i++) {
          value = value[path[i]];
        }
        return map(response, value);
      default:
        throw chunk.reason;
    }
  }
  ```

